---
title: BioPreDyn-bench B1 (Yeast Glycolysis) Parameter Estimation Benchmark
author: Arnav Kapoor
---

# Parameter estimation of a genome-scale yeast metabolic model

This benchmark implements problem **B1** from the
[BioPreDyn-bench suite](https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12918-015-0144-4)
(Villaverde et al. 2015), addressing
[SciMLBenchmarks.jl#555](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/555). B1 is the
largest problem in the suite: a genome-scale kinetic model of *Saccharomyces cerevisiae*
metabolism (Smallbone et al.), with 276 dynamic states (metabolite concentrations), 285
reactions, and **1770 parameters**, of which 1759 are estimated (the remaining 11 are known
constants: 9 fixed metabolite concentrations plus the `cell` and `extracellular`
compartment volumes). It is fit against 44 real, digitized time-course observables (38
metabolite concentrations and 6 reaction fluxes) sampled at 120 one-minute intervals during
a glucose-pulse experiment. As with [B2](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/blob/master/benchmarks/ParameterEstimation/BioPreDynB2ParameterEstimation.jmd)
this is real (not simulated) experimental data.

The model equations, nominal parameters, parameter bounds, and experimental data below are
transcribed directly from the benchmark's official MATLAB/AMIGO2 implementation, available
as [supplementary material](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4342829/) to the paper
(Additional files 2-3, directory `BioPreDynBenchFiles/B1`, file
`Matlab/dynamics_in_Matlab/b1_dyn_p.m`). Given the model's size, the 285 rate laws and 1147
nonzero stoichiometric coefficients are stored as flat lookup tables (below) rather than
written out reaction-by-reaction, but every entry was generated mechanically from the
reference file and cross-checked line-for-line against a literal re-implementation of it
(matching to machine precision on random test points).

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEq, Optimization, ForwardDiff
using OptimizationBBO, OptimizationNLopt, Plots, BenchmarkTools, DataFrames
using ParallelParticleSwarms
gr(fmt = :png)
```

## Model

Almost every one of the 285 reactions follows one of two forms found throughout the
reference model:

  - **Irreversible, single-substrate:** `rate = cell * Vmax * S / Km / (1 + S / Km)`.
  - **Reversible (possibly multi-substrate/-product), generalized Michaelis-Menten:**
    `rate = cell * Vmax * (∏Sᵢ^eᵢ - ∏Pⱼ^eⱼ / Keq) / ∏Kmᵢ^eᵢ / (∏(1 + Sᵢ/Kmᵢ)^eᵢ + ∏(1 + Pⱼ/Kmⱼ)^eⱼ - 1)`,
    where a reaction with no explicit `Keq` in the source uses `Keq = 1`.

One reaction (`r_2111`, protein synthesis) instead uses a log-linear form,
`rate = cell * max(V0 * (1 + Σᵢ εᵢ log(cᵢ / icᵢ)), 0)`, summing over 50 species. The
reference C implementation (`fmax` of an expression containing `log`) makes this reaction's
rate exactly 0 whenever any of its 50 species concentrations is non-positive -- a genuine
discontinuity in the model as published. Integrated literally, this discontinuity causes
the stiff solver to repeatedly reject steps near the boundary once trace species approach
zero (observed in development: step sizes collapsing to `~1e-9` with `MaxIters` reached
before `t = 15`). We use a continuous relaxation instead: the reaction ramps linearly to 0
as its smallest input concentration falls below a small floor `b1_eps`, and each logarithm
saturates at that same floor rather than diverging. This changes nothing while every
species exceeds `b1_eps`, and was checked to reproduce the same nominal-parameter cost, to
three significant figures, as the discontinuous version does wherever the latter can be
integrated to completion at all (both were tested down to `reltol = 1e-8`).

Every state obeys `dX/dt = (Σ stoichiometry * rate) / cell`. The 285 reactions and their
stoichiometric coefficients are transcribed as flat tables (`b1_S_*`) rather than as 276
separate equations, since the model is generated (originally from a genome-scale
reconstruction), not hand-derived.

```julia
const b1_nx = 276  # dynamic states
const b1_nr = 285  # reactions
const b1_eps = 1.0e-6  # concentration floor of the log-linear reaction (see b1_rate)

# Concentration of "species" `i` as referenced by the rate-law tables: 1..276 are the
# dynamic states, 277..285 are the nine fixed (known-constant) species stored as the 9
# known parameters `p[1760:1768]`, and 286 is the glucose input, carried as `p[1771]`.
@inline b1_conc(u, p, i) = i <= b1_nx ? u[i] : (i <= b1_nx + 9 ? p[1483 + i] : p[1771])

function b1_rate(k, u, p)
    T = promote_type(eltype(u), eltype(p))
    if k == b1_linlog_rxn
        # log-linear reaction; see the discussion above for the continuous relaxation.
        s = one(T)
        w = one(T)
        for j in eachindex(b1_linlog_sp)
            c = b1_conc(u, p, b1_linlog_sp[j])
            w = min(w, c / b1_eps)
            s += p[b1_linlog_ep[j]] * log(max(c, b1_eps) / p[b1_linlog_ic[j]])
        end
        return p[1769] * max(p[b1_linlog_v0] * s, zero(T)) * max(w, zero(T))
    end
    ns = one(T)
    ds = one(T)
    nk = one(T)
    for j in b1_sub_ptr[k]:(b1_sub_ptr[k + 1] - 1)
        c = b1_conc(u, p, b1_sub_sp[j])
        e = b1_sub_exp[j]
        km = p[b1_sub_km[j]]
        ns *= c^e
        ds *= (1 + c / km)^e
        nk *= km^e
    end
    if b1_prod_ptr[k + 1] == b1_prod_ptr[k]      # irreversible, no products
        return p[1769] * p[b1_vmax[k]] * ns / nk / ds
    end
    np = one(T)
    dp = one(T)
    for j in b1_prod_ptr[k]:(b1_prod_ptr[k + 1] - 1)
        c = b1_conc(u, p, b1_prod_sp[j])
        e = b1_prod_exp[j]
        np *= c^e
        dp *= (1 + c / p[b1_prod_km[j]])^e
    end
    keq = b1_keq[k] == 0 ? 1.0 : p[b1_keq[k]]
    return p[1769] * p[b1_vmax[k]] * (ns - np / keq) / nk / (ds + dp - 1)
end

function b1_kinetics!(du, u, p, t)
    fill!(du, zero(eltype(du)))
    for k in 1:b1_nr
        r = b1_rate(k, u, p)
        for j in b1_S_ptr[k]:(b1_S_ptr[k + 1] - 1)
            du[b1_S_row[j]] += b1_S_val[j] * r
        end
    end
    du ./= p[1769]
    return nothing
end
```

## Reaction and stoichiometry tables

`b1_vmax`/`b1_keq` give each reaction's `Vmax`/`Keq` parameter index (`0` when a reaction
has no `Keq`, i.e. an implicit `Keq = 1`). `b1_sub_*`/`b1_prod_*` are CSR-style tables:
reaction `k`'s substrates (respectively products) are entries `b1_sub_ptr[k]:(b1_sub_ptr[k+1]-1)`
of `b1_sub_sp`/`b1_sub_exp`/`b1_sub_km` (species index, stoichiometric exponent, `Km`
parameter index), and likewise for products. `b1_S_*` is the same CSR layout for the
276x285 stoichiometry matrix: reaction `k` updates states `b1_S_row[j]` by `b1_S_val[j]`
for `j` in `b1_S_ptr[k]:(b1_S_ptr[k+1]-1)`. `b1_linlog_rxn` is which reaction (by index)
uses the log-linear form, with its own `V0` parameter index and per-species
`(species, ic, ε)` index triples in `b1_linlog_sp`/`b1_linlog_ic`/`b1_linlog_ep`.

