const b1_obs_idx = [19, 39, 40, 52, 53, 79, 80, 87, 89, 91, 93, 112, 113, 114, 115, 116, 121, 132, 144, 156, 155, 157, 160, 165, 171, 180, 182, 196, 197, 217, 218, 239, 246, 261, 265, 266, 268, 269]
const b1_flux_rxn = [272, 277, 280, 270, 273, 284]

const b1_err = max.(0.2 .* b1_data, 1.0e-6)
```

## Objective function

For a candidate parameter vector, we solve the two glucose segments in sequence (feeding
segment 1's final state in as segment 2's initial condition), read off the 38 observed
state concentrations and evaluate the 6 observed fluxes via `b1_rate` at each of the 120
time points, and sum the squared, `b1_err`-weighted residuals against `b1_data` --
reproduced here exactly from the AMIGO2 script `b1_obj.m`. A `DomainError` (a negative
concentration raised to a non-integer power inside a rate law) or a failed integration is
penalized with a large constant cost rather than propagated, matching this folder's other
BioPreDyn-bench benchmarks.

```julia
b1_prob = ODEProblem(b1_kinetics!, b1_y0, (0.0, 10.0), zeros(1771))

function biopredyn_b1_cost(θ)
    p_pulse = vcat(θ, b1_known, 74.0)
    p_chase = vcat(θ, b1_known, 1.0)
    try
        sol1 = solve(b1_prob, FBDF(); p = p_pulse, saveat = 0.0:1.0:10.0,
            reltol = 1.0e-6, abstol = 1.0e-10, maxiters = 20_000)
        sol1.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return 1.0e20
        prob2 = remake(b1_prob; u0 = sol1.u[end], tspan = (10.0, 119.0), p = p_chase)
        sol2 = solve(prob2, FBDF(); saveat = 10.0:1.0:119.0,
            reltol = 1.0e-6, abstol = 1.0e-10, maxiters = 20_000)
        sol2.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return 1.0e20

        ms = Matrix{Float64}(undef, 120, 44)
        for row in 1:120
            u = row <= 10 ? sol1.u[row] : sol2.u[row - 10]
            p = row <= 10 ? p_pulse : p_chase
            for (c, i) in enumerate(b1_obs_idx)
                ms[row, c] = u[i]
            end
            for (c, k) in enumerate(b1_flux_rxn)
                ms[row, 38 + c] = b1_rate(k, u, p)
            end
        end
        cost = sum(abs2, (ms .- b1_data) ./ b1_err)
        return isfinite(cost) ? cost : 1.0e20
    catch e
        e isa DomainError || rethrow()
        return 1.0e20
    end
end
```

```julia
@time cost_nominal = biopredyn_b1_cost(b1_p_nom)
@time cost_start = biopredyn_b1_cost(b1_p_start)
```

The original study reports reaching a cost of `Jf ≈ 1.3753·10⁴` using the enhanced scatter
search (eSS) global optimizer after ~7·10⁵ function evaluations (about 170 CPU-hours on an
Intel Xeon Quadcore, 2.50 GHz) -- by far the most expensive benchmark in this suite, and a
useful reminder of the scale of a genome-scale kinetic model. As with B5, the reference
files describe `b1_p_nom` only as a "nominal or best known" parameter set: evaluating the
cost at `b1_p_nom` does not reproduce the data to within the stated measurement noise, so
it is a reference point rather than the exact parameters that generated the data. We treat
`Jf ≈ 1.3753·10⁴` as the reference cost to reach.

## Visualizing the fit at nominal and starting parameters

```julia
sol1_nom = solve(b1_prob, FBDF(); p = vcat(b1_p_nom, b1_known, 74.0), reltol = 1.0e-6,
    abstol = 1.0e-10, saveat = 0.0:0.25:10.0)
prob2_nom = remake(b1_prob; u0 = sol1_nom.u[end], tspan = (10.0, 119.0),
    p = vcat(b1_p_nom, b1_known, 1.0))
sol2_nom = solve(prob2_nom, FBDF(); reltol = 1.0e-6, abstol = 1.0e-10, saveat = 10.0:0.25:119.0)

plot(sol1_nom, idxs = b1_obs_idx[1:4], label = permutedims(string.(b1_obs_idx[1:4])),
    xlabel = "time (min)", title = "B1 dynamics at nominal parameters (glucose pulse, t < 10)")
```

```julia
plot(sol2_nom, idxs = b1_obs_idx[1:4], label = permutedims(string.(b1_obs_idx[1:4])),
    xlabel = "time (min)", title = "B1 dynamics at nominal parameters (t >= 10)")
```

## Parameter estimation

We benchmark global optimizers on this 1759-parameter problem, starting from `b1_p_start`
(the reference implementation's own fixed starting guess, taken from its
`example_optimization/b1_bounds.mat`) and using the bounds given above. As with B2, B4 and
B5, we include `BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin`, `GN_CRS2_LM`, and `ParallelPSOArray` from
[ParallelParticleSwarms.jl](https://github.com/SciML/ParallelParticleSwarms.jl). Given the
size of this problem (each cost evaluation solves a 276-state, mildly stiff ODE system
twice), we use a smaller evaluation budget than the other BioPreDyn-bench benchmarks in
this folder. Each trackable optimizer's raw per-evaluation cost is recorded via a callback,
from which we compute the running best-found cost for a convergence plot;
`ParallelPSOArray` does not yet support the `callback` keyword, so only its final result is
available.

```julia
optf = OptimizationFunction((p, _) -> biopredyn_b1_cost(p))
optprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, b1_p_start, lb = p_lower, ub = p_upper)
```

```julia
losses_bbo = Float64[]
times_bbo = Float64[]
t0_bbo = time()
cb_bbo = (state, l) -> (push!(losses_bbo, l); push!(times_bbo, time() - t0_bbo); false)
@time res_bbo = solve(
    optprob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(), maxiters = 2000, callback = cb_bbo)
res_bbo.objective
```

```julia
losses_nlopt = Float64[]
times_nlopt = Float64[]
t0_nlopt = time()
cb_nlopt = (state, l) -> (push!(losses_nlopt, l); push!(times_nlopt, time() - t0_nlopt); false)
opt = Opt(:GN_CRS2_LM, length(b1_p_nom))
@time res_nlopt = solve(optprob, opt, maxiters = 2000, callback = cb_nlopt)
res_nlopt.objective
```

```julia
n_particles = 40
pso_iters = 1200 ÷ n_particles
@time res_pso = solve(optprob, ParallelPSOArray(n_particles), maxiters = pso_iters)
res_pso.objective
```

## Local refinement

Global metaheuristics are good at finding a promising basin but slow to fine-tune within
it; the original study's own eSS method is itself a *hybrid* global+local algorithm for
exactly this reason. We polish each of the three global results above with `LN_BOBYQA`
(derivative-free).

```julia
function polish(start_u, label)
    prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, start_u, lb = p_lower, ub = p_upper)
    losses = Float64[]
    times = Float64[]
    t0 = time()
    cb = (state, l) -> (push!(losses, l); push!(times, time() - t0); false)
    t = @elapsed res = solve(
        prob, Opt(:LN_BOBYQA, length(b1_p_nom)), maxiters = 500, callback = cb)
    println(label, ": ", res.objective, " (", t, "s)")
    return res, losses, times
end

res_bbo_polish, losses_bbo_polish, times_bbo_polish = polish(res_bbo.u, "BBO -> LN_BOBYQA")
res_nlopt_polish, losses_nlopt_polish, times_nlopt_polish = polish(
    res_nlopt.u, "GN_CRS2_LM -> LN_BOBYQA")
res_pso_polish, losses_pso_polish, times_pso_polish = polish(
    res_pso.u, "ParallelPSOArray -> LN_BOBYQA")
nothing
```

```julia
df = DataFrame(
    method = ["Reference nominal parameters", "Starting guess (no fit)",
        "BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin", "GN_CRS2_LM", "ParallelPSOArray",
        "LN_BOBYQA polish (from BBO)", "LN_BOBYQA polish (from GN_CRS2_LM)",
        "LN_BOBYQA polish (from ParallelPSOArray)"],
    cost = [cost_nominal, cost_start, res_bbo.objective, res_nlopt.objective,
        res_pso.objective, res_bbo_polish.objective, res_nlopt_polish.objective,
        res_pso_polish.objective])
```

## Convergence

```julia
bestcost_bbo = accumulate(min, losses_bbo)
bestcost_nlopt = accumulate(min, losses_nlopt)
bestcost_bbo_polish = accumulate(min, losses_bbo_polish)
bestcost_nlopt_polish = accumulate(min, losses_nlopt_polish)
bestcost_pso_polish = accumulate(min, losses_pso_polish)

pso_evals = n_particles * pso_iters
bbo_polish_iters = length(losses_bbo) .+ (1:length(losses_bbo_polish))
nlopt_polish_iters = length(losses_nlopt) .+ (1:length(losses_nlopt_polish))
pso_polish_iters = pso_evals .+ (1:length(losses_pso_polish))

plot(bestcost_bbo, label = "BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin", yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "iteration", ylabel = "best cost so far (log scale)",
    title = "B1 parameter estimation convergence", legend = :outertopright,
    size = (900, 500))
plot!(bestcost_nlopt, label = "GN_CRS2_LM")
plot!(bbo_polish_iters, bestcost_bbo_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from BBO)")
plot!(nlopt_polish_iters, bestcost_nlopt_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from GN_CRS2_LM)")
plot!(pso_polish_iters, bestcost_pso_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from PSO)")
hline!([13753.0], label = "paper reference (eSS, ~7x10^5 evals)", linestyle = :dash)
```

Iteration count alone hides that these optimizers have very different per-iteration costs,
so we also plot the same running-best costs against wall-clock seconds, with each curve
measured from its own start:

```julia
plot(times_bbo, bestcost_bbo, label = "BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin", yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "wall-clock time (s)", ylabel = "best cost so far (log scale)",
    title = "B1 parameter estimation convergence (wall time)", legend = :outertopright,
    size = (900, 500))
plot!(times_nlopt, bestcost_nlopt, label = "GN_CRS2_LM")
plot!(times_bbo_polish, bestcost_bbo_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from BBO)")
plot!(times_nlopt_polish, bestcost_nlopt_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from GN_CRS2_LM)")
plot!(times_pso_polish, bestcost_pso_polish, label = "LN_BOBYQA polish (from PSO)")
hline!([13753.0], label = "paper reference (eSS, ~7x10^5 evals)", linestyle = :dash)
```

`ParallelPSOArray`'s own result isn't shown as a curve (no per-iteration history is
available, as noted above), but its polish curve starts from wherever `res_pso.objective`
landed, plotted alongside the other two polish curves for direct comparison. As in the B5
benchmark, each polish curve in the iteration plot is offset by its own global run's
evaluation count (`ParallelPSOArray`'s using particles x iterations, since it records no
history).

# Conclusion

This benchmark demonstrates SciML's parameter estimation stack (`OrdinaryDiffEq` +
`Optimization.jl`) on BioPreDyn-bench B1, the largest problem in the suite: a genome-scale,
1759-parameter, 276-state kinetic model of yeast glycolysis fit against real experimental
data. Unlike the smaller problems in this folder, a single cost evaluation here is itself
a nontrivial computation (two stiff-ODE solves of a 276-state system), and the reference
study needed ~7·10⁵ evaluations (170 CPU-hours) to reach its reported optimum -- several
orders of magnitude more than is practical to reproduce in a CI benchmark, so the runs here
use a correspondingly smaller evaluation budget and are best read as a demonstration that
the stack scales to a problem of this size, rather than as a competitive attempt at the
literature reference of `Jf ≈ 1.3753·10⁴`. As with B5, the reference nominal parameters are
a point of comparison, not the exact parameters that generated the data (see above).

A genuine discontinuity in the published model (one reaction's rate law drops to exactly 0
whenever any of its 50 inputs is non-positive) causes the stiff integrator to stall once
trace species approach zero; we resolve this with a continuous relaxation of that single
reaction (see "Model" above) rather than by loosening solver tolerances, since loosening
tolerances alone does not resolve the underlying discontinuity and was observed to change
the computed cost by an order of magnitude between successive tolerance levels.

